ng) to maximize the minimum area of boundary-only triangles; second, the 3 interior points are placed at non-lattice positions to avoid lattice alignment; third, the entire configuration is given a small irrational perturbation (~0.01) to break all symmetries. The minimum triangle area is 1/144.", "probability": 0.05}, {"conjecture": "The optimal configuration has exactly 7 boundary points (all 4 corners plus 3 edge midpoints, with one edge having no midpoint) and 6 interior points arranged in a perturbed hexagonal pattern (regular hexagon with center, scaled to radius ~0.3, with each point perturbed by ~0.04 in a direction tangent to the hexagon). The hexagonal interior pattern avoids rectangular lattice alignment, and the asymmetric boundary (missing one edge midpoint) breaks the D4 symmetry. The minimum triangle area is 1/130.", "probability": 0.04}, {"conjecture": "All three error modes can be simultaneously addressed by a configuration where 5 points lie on the boundary (4 corners plus 1 edge midpoint) and 8 points lie in the interior at the vertices of a perturbed regular octagon (radius ~0.32, rotation by arctan(1/3), with each vertex perturbed radially by ~0.02-0.03). The octagonal structure avoids lattice alignment, the single edge midpoint breaks bilateral symmetry, and the radial perturbations break the octagonal rotational symmetry. The minimum triangle area is 1/126.", "probability": 0.03}]}
